rate a fine polygonal substructure during continuous rolling. [Data: Entity (275); Relationship (1510)]\"},{\"summary\":\"Recovery-region rolling can produce abnormal coarse grains\",\"explanation\":\"In the recovery region, most austenite grains undergo limited structural change because stored deformation energy is released primarily through recovery. Nevertheless, strain-induced grain-boundary migration can produce locally very coarse grains while neighboring grains remain comparatively unchanged. [Data: Entity (364); Relationships (455, 457)]\\n\\nThe network further identifies huge grains that exceed the initial grain size and can persist through subsequent rolling, including partial-recrystallization-region passes. These grains may transform to upper bainite. [Data: Entity (375); Relationship (462)]\"},{\"summary\":\"Repeated deformation in the recovery region may leave recrystallization incomplete\",\"explanation\":\"Repeated deformation in the recovery region is associated with sluggish and incomplete recrystallization. This creates a processing condition in which the intended refinement response may not fully develop. [Data: Entity (382); Relationship (484)]\\n\\nThe supplied schedules of 10% deformation per pass over three passes, totaling 28%, and over five passes, totaling 42%, are specifically linked to changes in austenite grain structure during recovery-region rolling in Nb steel. [Data: Entities (399, 400); Relationships (528, 529)]\"},{\"summary\":\"Static-recrystallization nucleation and front migration govern refinement\",\"explanation\":\"Static recrystallization nucleates predominantly at grain boundaries, with triple junctions identified as predominant nucleation sites. Nucleation is localized and inhomogeneous within the deformed matrix. [Data: Entities (309, 310, 351, 311); Relationships (367, 427, 369)]\\n\\nAfter nucleation, the recrystallizing front migrates into the deformed matrix. This provides the described mechanism connecting nucleati